-- title:
--   A thin double cone whose lattice points lie deep inside a given double cone
-- statement:
--   Let $d \ge 2$, let $r > 0$, let $u \in \mathbb{R}^d$ be a unit vector, and let $\vartheta$ satisfy $0 < \vartheta \le \pi/2$. Write $V(w,\theta) = \tilde V(w,\theta)\cup -\tilde V(w,\theta)$ for the double cone with axis $w$ and apex angle $\theta$, and write
--
--   $$S_r = \{\, y \in S : \overline{B}(y,r) \subseteq S \,\}$$
--
--   for the $r$-shrinking of a set $S$, i.e. the points of $S$ at depth at least $r$ inside it. Then there exist a unit vector $v$ and an angle $\theta$ with
--
--   $$0 < \theta \le \vartheta/4$$
--
--   such that every lattice point of the thin double cone $V(v,\theta)$ is a lattice point lying at depth $r$ inside the given double cone $V(u,\vartheta)$:
--
--   $$V(v,\theta) \cap \mathbb{Z}^d \;\subseteq\; \bigl(V(u,\vartheta)\bigr)_r \cap \mathbb{Z}^d .$$
--
--   Note that the inclusion is asserted only for *lattice* points: the thin cone need not be contained in the shrunken wide cone as a set of real points, only its integer points must be.
--
--   Thinness — an aperture at most a quarter of the original — combined with depth $r$ is what allows a lattice point selected in the thin cone to be perturbed by up to $r$ without leaving the original cone; the restriction to lattice points is precisely the weakening that makes the statement true near the apex, where no thin cone can be deep inside a wide one.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** $\vartheta$ is not required to be related to $\theta$ beyond $\theta \le \vartheta/4$, and $v$ is not required to be close to $u$ in the statement. The hypothesis $d \ge 2$ is essential: in dimension one every double cone with positive aperture is all of $\mathbb{R}\setminus\{0\}$ and no shrinking of it contains a neighbourhood of the excluded origin.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/dbenbenn/quadratic-forms-sobolev/blob/7a1a680db2124d46ce370c91fd450aa454edf491/QuadraticFormsSobolev/ThinCones.lean#L106-L230

